00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
these teenagers are some of the 5 000  members of a youth movement in the  pro-russia separatist controlled region  of eastern ukraine  they are all still at school  unami roughly translates to army of the  youth  instructors many of them former  separatist fighters say they teach  patriotism and how to defend the  self-declared republic of donetsk  which after eight years of conflict with  ukraine remains unrecognized by any  country in the world even its main  backer russia  the students are taught a range of  skills including martial arts first aid  and assembling and disassembling weapons  which we are not allowed to film  fourteen-year-old christina joined two  years ago after her cousin was killed  during fighting between the  russian-backed separatists and the  ukrainian army  it's very painful for me when i remember  he was like my brother  i joined because of his death because i  wanted to remember those lost in the war  especially  we don't want to make the same mistakes  that we did when the soviet union was  attacked in world war ii  murals in the school corridors celebrate  russian history and cultural figures  like the novelist and poet alexander  pushkin  a map shows crimea painted in the  colours of the russian flag  today's class is about the victory of  soviet forces over the nazis in the  battle of stalingrad considered one of  the bloodiest battles in history  claiming around 2 million lives  feels like my second family my second  home when you go through so much  together you become very close  it develops our patriotism we remember  and are proud of our predecessors i want  to eventually serve in the army  outside the students prepare for a mock  battle  they split into two opposing sides and  fire at each other using laser guns and  stacks of tyres as cover  but this  isn't just a game  this might look like fun but of course  it has serious implications and it's  important to recognize that there are  similar youth groups on the ukrainian  government control side also being  trained like this there they are taught  that the danger is what the ukrainians  describe as a russian expansionism with  its roots in the soviet past  here instructors tell the children that  the danger is an ideology that has huge  resonance with both the young and the  older  ukraine is not our enemy fascism is the  enemy in 2014 when the conflict started  we were standing up against fascists  like our grandparents did we can't say  we have enemies our only enemy is  fascism we don't want to adjust to make  fascism our friend  christina and her friends are too young  to fully understand how history can be  manipulated to serve military or  political ends in any conflict  a few kilometers away are the abandoned  ruins of homes and places where children  once played destroyed in eight years of  war with no peaceful solution in sight a  conflict into which the young on both  sides are being drawn  charles stratford al-jazeera donetsk
 

